Mart Parve commented: 

Armin, your product assumptions are correct. 

As for “why this solution”, then we don’t support the “individual credit cards for employees of one Business customer” case. This is planned for 2021 but it’s a tricky project and thus impossible before EOQ1, and probably more like Q2-Q4. 

Currently, the scenarios contemplated by Ops are explicitly  *not supported* by Product: 
{color}
* {color:#333333}Business Portal with individual credit cards: Not working at all 
{color}
* {color:#333333}Many Business Portal accounts for one Company (one for each employee): Will break reporting in some places; suspect to fraud check false positives; no central rides reporting; not possible to identify which invoice documents belong to which user and on our end which company belongs to which employee (unless user name or code added to the legal Company Name); possibly other drawbacks and risks. With Business Portal, we have at least email validation, thus we have control over who is enrolling in the scheme. 
{color}
* {color:#333333}“Affiliated” Work Profiles (Customer supplying a list of enrolled email addresses, us filtering the rides for these accounts and calculating kickbacks): We have no way to control who is enrolling in the scheme; it’s very easy to mistakenly select a personal payment method for Work Profile rides, and we have no way of making it right retroactively. 


While it is conceivable to go for an “ops hack” here with either of the last 2 options, it’s risky one way or another.  *Thus, legally the service should be “as is”, and commercially, there’s a risk of customer dissatisfaction.* 

Also, with the work profile hack, we have no watertight method of controlling who is enrolling in the scheme. I can’t see any incentive to misrepresent oneself in the portal as a PWC France, but just that there is no technical control over that. 
{color}
